###############################################################################
# requests.http — classroom demo script
#
# Open this in VS Code with the "REST Client" extension and click "Send Request"
# above each block. Run them top to bottom during the session; keep
# src/data/user.json open in a split pane so students watch it change.
###############################################################################

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000

### 0. API index — see every endpoint at a glance
GET {{baseUrl}}/

###############################################################################
# PART A — OPEN ROUTES (no middleware). Plain REST CRUD.
###############################################################################

### A1. CREATE a user -> expect 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/users
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Asha Rao", "email": "asha@example.com", "age": 24 }

### A2. CREATE another -> expect 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/users
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Ravi Kumar", "email": "ravi@example.com", "age": 31 }

### A3. CREATE with a duplicate email -> expect 409 Conflict
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/users
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Impostor", "email": "asha@example.com" }

### A4. CREATE with bad input -> expect 400 with a list of errors
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/users
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "A", "email": "not-an-email" }

### A5. READ all users -> expect 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/users

### A6. READ one user -> expect 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/users/1

### A7. READ a user that doesn't exist -> expect 404
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/users/999

### A8. UPDATE a user -> expect 200, note updatedAt changes
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/users/1
Content-Type: application/json

{ "age": 25 }

### A9. DELETE a user -> expect 200
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/users/2

### A10. DELETE the same user again -> expect 404.
###      Server state is identical after both calls: DELETE is idempotent.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/users/2

###############################################################################
# PART B — PROTECTED ROUTES (with the isLoggedIn middleware).
# Same controllers as Part A. Only the middleware differs.
###############################################################################

### B1. Check the session -> isLoggedIn should be false
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/status

### B2. Try a protected route while logged OUT -> expect 401.
###     The controller never runs; the middleware stopped the chain.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/secure/users

### B3. LOG IN -> flips isLoggedIn to true
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login

### B4. Same request as B2, now logged in -> expect 200.
###     Nothing about the controller changed. Only the guard passed.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/secure/users

### B5. CREATE through the protected route -> expect 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/secure/users
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Meera Nair", "email": "meera@example.com", "age": 29 }

### B6. READ one through the protected route -> expect 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/secure/users/1

### B7. UPDATE through the protected route -> expect 200
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/secure/users/1
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Asha R. Rao", "age": 26 }

### B8. DELETE through the protected route -> expect 200
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/secure/users/1

### B9. LOG OUT
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout

### B10. Every protected route is now 401 again -> the payoff moment
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/secure/users
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Nobody", "email": "nobody@example.com" }

###############################################################################
# PART C — error handling
###############################################################################

### C1. Unknown route -> expect 404 from the notFound middleware
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/does-not-exist

### C2. Malformed JSON -> expect 400 from the central error handler
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/users
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "broken",
